                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* Heterozygous parent :\u202f\\(ABC/abc\\) (cis\u2011configuration)  \n* Tester :\u202f\\(abc/abc\\) \u2013 therefore the genotype of every offspring is simply the gamete contributed by the heterozygote together with \\(abc\\).  \n\n*Viability rule* \u2013 an offspring that is **homozygous recessive at both A and C** (\\(a/a\\;c/c\\)) survives with probability \\(\u00bd\\); all other genotypes survive with probability\u202f1.  \n\nOnly the two gamete types that contain **both a and c** create the lethal genotype:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{abc}\\;(a\\,b\\,c)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{aBc}\\;(a\\,B\\,c)}\n\\]\n\nAll other six gamete types survive completely.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Correct the observed survivor numbers\n\n| Gamete | Survivors (raw) | Viability factor | Zygotes (corrected) |\n|--------|-----------------|------------------|---------------------|\n| **ABC**| 344 | 1 | 344 |\n| **abc**| 170 | \u00bd | \\(170\\times2 = 340\\) |\n| **Abc**| 80  | 1 | 80 |\n| **aBC**| 80  | 1 | 80 |\n| **ABc**| 70  | 1 | 70 |\n| **abC**| 70  | 1 | 70 |\n| **AbC**| 8   | 1 | 8 |\n| **aBc**| 4   | \u00bd | \\(4\\times2 = 8\\) |\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Total corrected zygotes}=344+340+80+80+70+70+8+8=1000}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Parental and double\u2011crossover (DCO) classes \u2013 gene order\n\n*Parental (non\u2011recombinant) gametes*: the two that match the original haplotypes  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ABC\\quad\\text{and}\\quad abc}\n\\]\n\n*Double\u2011crossover gametes*: the two that have the outer markers in the parental arrangement but the middle marker flipped  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AbC\\quad\\text{and}\\quad aBc}\n\\]\n\nBecause the DCO classes differ only at the **middle** locus, the middle gene must be **B**.  \nHence the linear order is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A\\;-\\;B\\;-\\;C}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Recombination fractions (RF) and map distances for the two adjacent intervals  \n\nTotal zygotes \\(N=1000\\).\n\n#### Interval A\u2013B  \n\nRecombinants are those in which A and B are **not** in coupling (AB or ab):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Recombinant classes}&=Abc,\\;aBC,\\;AbC,\\;aBc\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Count}&=80+80+8+8=176\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\displaystyle RF_{A\\!-\\!B}= \\frac{176}{1000}=0.176\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\\boxed{17.6\\;\\text{cM}}\n\\]\n\n#### Interval B\u2013C  \n\nRecombinants are those in which B and C are not in coupling (BC or bc):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Recombinant classes}&=ABc,\\;abC,\\;AbC,\\;aBc\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Count}&=70+70+8+8=156\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\displaystyle RF_{B\\!-\\!C}= \\frac{156}{1000}=0.156\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\\boxed{15.6\\;\\text{cM}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Direct recombination between the outer genes A and C  \n\nOuter\u2011gene recombinants are those in which A and C are not in coupling (AC or ac):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Recombinant classes}&=Abc,\\;aBC,\\;ABc,\\;abC\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Count}&=80+80+70+70=300\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\displaystyle RF_{A\\!-\\!C}= \\frac{300}{1000}=0.30\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\\boxed{30\\;\\text{cM}}\n\\]\n\n**Why \\(RF_{A\\!-\\!C}\\neq RF_{A\\!-\\!B}+RF_{B\\!-\\!C}\\)?**  \n\nA double crossover restores the parental relationship between the outer markers (A and C) while flipping the middle marker. Consequently the two DCO classes (**AbC**, **aBc**) are counted as *parental* for the A\u2013C pair, lowering the observed A\u2013C recombination fraction below the simple sum of the two adjacent intervals (0.176\u202f+\u202f0.156\u202f=\u202f0.332).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Coefficient of coincidence & interference (using corrected data)\n\nAssuming independence of crossovers in the two intervals,\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{DCO})_{\\text{exp}} = RF_{A\\!-\\!B}\\times RF_{B\\!-\\!C}=0.176\\times0.156=0.027456 .\n\\]\n\nExpected number of DCO zygotes  \n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{DCO}} = N\\times P(\\text{DCO})_{\\text{exp}} = 1000\\times0.027456 = 27.46\\;(\u224827.5).\n\\]\n\nObserved DCO zygotes  \n\n\\[\nO_{\\text{DCO}} = AbC + aBc = 8+8 = 16 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Coefficient of coincidence } C = \\frac{O_{\\text{DCO}}}{E_{\\text{DCO}}}= \\frac{16}{27.46}=0.58}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Interference } I = 1-C = 0.42}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.   \u201cUncorrected\u2019\u2019 analysis (use the raw survivor numbers)\n\nTotal survivors \\(N_{\\text{obs}} = 826\\).\n\n| Interval | Recombinant classes (counts) | RF (uncorrected) |\n|----------|------------------------------|-------------------|\n| A\u2013B      | Abc\u202f80, aBC\u202f80, AbC\u202f8, aBc\u202f4 | \\(\\dfrac{172}{826}=0.208\\) |\n| B\u2013C      | ABc\u202f70, abC\u202f70, AbC\u202f8, aBc\u202f4 | \\(\\dfrac{152}{826}=0.184\\) |\n| A\u2013C      | Abc\u202f80, aBC\u202f80, ABc\u202f70, abC\u202f70 | \\(\\dfrac{300}{826}=0.363\\) |\n\nMap distances (cM): 20.8, 18.4, 36.3 respectively.\n\n**DCO (uncorrected)**  \n\nObserved DCO = AbC\u202f8 + aBc\u202f4 = 12  \n\nExpected DCO = \\(N_{\\text{obs}}\\times RF_{A\\!-\\!B}^{\\text{obs}}\\times RF_{B\\!-\\!C}^{\\text{obs}} = 826\\times0.208\\times0.184 = 31.7\\).\n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{obs}} = \\frac{12}{31.7}=0.38,\\qquad\nI_{\\text{obs}} = 1-0.38 = 0.62 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.   Comparison & explanation of the distortion\n\n| Quantity | Corrected (after viability) | Uncorrected (raw survivors) |\n|----------|-----------------------------|------------------------------|\n| Total zygotes | 1000 | 826 |\n| RF\u202f(A\u2011B) | 0.176 (17.6\u202fcM) | 0.208 (20.8\u202fcM) |\n| RF\u202f(B\u2011C) | 0.156 (15.6\u202fcM) | 0.184 (18.4\u202fcM) |\n| RF\u202f(A\u2011C) | 0.300 (30\u202fcM)   | 0.363 (36.3\u202fcM) |\n| DCO count | 16 | 12 |\n| Coincidence | 0.58 | 0.38 |\n| Interference | 0.42 | 0.62 |\n\n**What causes the differences?**  \n\n* The lethal genotype \\(a/a\\;c/c\\) arises only from the two gametes **abc** and **aBc**.  \n* Both of these gametes are **parental** for the A\u2013B interval (they keep the AB coupling) but **recombinant** for the A\u2013C interval (they separate a from c).  \n* Because half of the zygotes derived from these two gametes die, their contribution to the observed pool is halved.  \n\nConsequences:\n\n1. **Parental class \u201cabc\u201d is under\u2011represented**, inflating the apparent proportion of recombinants for any interval that uses A or C as a marker.  \n2. **Class \u201caBc\u201d is also under\u2011represented**, removing a substantial number of recombinants for the A\u2013C and B\u2013C intervals simultaneously.  \n3. The net effect is a systematic **over\u2011estimate of recombination fractions** when the raw survivor numbers are used, especially for the outer\u2011gene pair (A\u2013C) where the loss of the aBc class removes many recombinants.  \n\nSince the selection acts **after fertilisation**, it cannot be distinguished from a genuine change in meiotic behaviour. The experiment therefore **mistakes post\u2011fertilisation viability bias for altered meiotic recombination**, producing apparently higher recombination rates and a larger apparent interference (lower coincidence) than truly exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary of key numerical answers**\n\n1. Corrected zygote numbers (total\u202f=\u202f1000) \u2013 see table in section\u202f1.  \n2. Parental gametes: **ABC**, **abc**; double\u2011crossover gametes: **AbC**, **aBc**; gene order **A\u2013B\u2013C**.  \n3. \\(RF_{A\\!-\\!B}=0.176\\;(17.6\\text{ cM})\\);\u2003\\(RF_{B\\!-\\!C}=0.156\\;(15.6\\text{ cM})\\).  \n4. \\(RF_{A\\!-\\!C}=0.30\\;(30\\text{ cM})\\); it is smaller than the sum of the two adjacent RFs because double crossovers restore the parental A\u2013C relationship.  \n5. Expected DCO\u202f\u2248\u202f27.5; observed DCO\u202f=\u202f16 \u2192 \\(C=0.58\\), \\(I=0.42\\).  \n6. Using raw survivors: \\(RF_{A\\!-\\!B}=0.208\\), \\(RF_{B\\!-\\!C}=0.184\\), \\(RF_{A\\!-\\!C}=0.363\\); \\(C=0.38\\), \\(I=0.62\\).  \n7. The viability penalty selectively eliminates the **abc** and **aBc** gametes, biasing the observed frequencies upward for recombination involving A or C and creating the illusion of greater interference.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer many subquestions. Let's parse data.\n\nParent heterozygous: ABC/abc (cis). Testcross to abc/abc yields gametes from heterozygote as above. Without selection, we would expect Mendelian frequencies based on recombination.\n\nBut there is viability selection: offspring that are homozygous recessive at both A and C (i.e., a/a ; c/c) survive with prob 0.5, others survive with prob 1. Which gamete classes lead to such genotype? Since testcross partner provides abc allele, the offspring genotype will have one allele from heterozygote (the gamete) and one from tester (abc). So if heterozygote contributes a gamete containing a and c alleles (i.e., gamete includes a and c), then offspring will be a/a ; c/c (since tester also gives a and c). That means any gamete that carries both a and c (regardless of B) leads to a/a ; c/c. The gamete types with a and c: those where allele at A = a, allele at C = c. Looking at list:\n\nGamete strings: ABC (A,B,C)\nabc (a,b,c)\nAbc (A,b,c)\naBC (a,B,C)\nABc (A,B,c)\nabC (a,b,C)\nAbC (A,b,C)\naBc (a,B,c)\n\nWhich have a and c? Those with a and c: abc (a,b,c) yes; aBc (a,B,c) yes; abC (a,b,C) no because C=C not c; aBC (a,B,C) no; etc. Also maybe Abc (A,b,c) has c but not a, so not both. So only abc and aBc have both a and c. Wait also maybe \"abC\" has a and C (dominant) not c. So only two classes: abc and aBc.\n\nThus these two classes suffer viability penalty (survival 0.5). Others survive fully.\n\nNow we have observed survivors counts:\n\nABC 344\nabc 170\nAbc 80\naBC 80\nABc 70\nabC 70\nAbC 8\naBc 4\n\nTotal survivors = sum = let's compute: 344+170=514; +80=594; +80=674; +70=744; +70=814; +8=822; +4=826. Total survivors = 826.\n\nNow we need to correct for viability: For classes without penalty, observed survivors = true zygotes. For penalized classes (abc and aBc), observed survivors = 0.5 * true zygotes. Therefore true zygotes = observed / 0.5 = observed *2.\n\nSo corrected counts:\n\nABC: 344 (no penalty)\nabc: 170*2 = 340\nAbc: 80\naBC: 80\nABc: 70\nabC: 70\nAbC: 8\naBc: 4*2 = 8\n\nCheck totals: sum = 344+340=684; +80=764; +80=844; +70=914; +70=984; +8=992; +8=1000. So corrected total zygotes = 1000. Good.\n\nNow part 1 done.\n\nPart 2: Identify parental classes and double crossover classes. Parental haplotypes are ABC and abc (non-recombinant). In testcross, parental gametes correspond to non-recombinant: ABC and abc. Indeed they are most frequent (344 and 340). Double crossover classes are those that result from two crossovers, giving recombinant combination of outer markers while inner marker reverts to parental state. Need gene order.\n\nWe have three loci A-B-C. We need to determine order based on which interval shows higher recombination. Typically double crossovers involve swapping of middle marker relative to outer ones. The double crossover class will have the same outer markers as parental but middle opposite. For example, if order is A-B-C, parental ABC and abc. Double crossover would give AbC and aBc (outer A and C same as parental, middle B flipped). Indeed our low-frequency classes are AbC (8) and aBc (8). These are symmetric and low count, consistent with double crossovers. So gene order likely A-B-C (or C-B-A). But we need to decide orientation. Since parental are ABC and abc, double crossovers are AbC and aBc. That matches order A-B-C (with B in middle). So gene order: A\u2014B\u2014C.\n\nAlternatively could be C-B-A, but then double crossovers would be ACb and aCb? Not present. So order A-B-C.\n\nNow part 3: Compute recombination fraction (RF) for each adjacent interval.\n\nFirst interval A-B: RF_AB = (number of recombinant gametes for A vs B)/total zygotes. Recombinants are those where A and B are not in coupling (i.e., AB vs ab). Coupling parental: ABC and abc (both have A with B together). Recombinant classes: Abc (A b c), aBC (a B C), ABc (A B c?) Wait ABc has A B c, that's still coupling? Actually coupling refers to A with B same phase: either AB or ab. So ABc has A B (coupling) though C different. That's not recombinant for A-B. Similarly abC has a b (coupling). So recombinant for A-B are those where A and B are opposite: Abc (A b), aBC (a B). Also possibly AbC? That's A b C (still A b). But AbC also has A b, so it's recombinant for A-B. And aBc also a B. So total recombinant for A-B = Abc + aBC + AbC + aBc = 80+80+8+8 = 176. Total zygotes = 1000. So RF_AB = 176/1000 = 0.176. Map distance = 17.6 cM (approx).\n\nSecond interval B-C: Recombinants are those where B and C are not in coupling (i.e., BC vs bc). Parental coupling: ABC (B C), abc (b c). Recombinant classes: ABc (A B c) -> B c (recombinant); abC (a b C) -> b C (recombinant); Abc (A b c) -> b c (coupling) not recombinant; aBC (a B C) -> B C (coupling). Also AbC (A b C) -> b C (recombinant); aBc (a B c) -> B c (recombinant). So recombinant classes: ABc, abC, AbC, aBc = 70+70+8+8 = 156. RF_BC = 156/1000 = 0.156 => 15.6 cM.\n\nCheck consistency: Sum of adjacent distances = 33.2 cM, less than overall distance perhaps due to double crossovers.\n\nNow part 4: Recombination fraction between outer genes A and C directly. Recombinants are those where A and C are not in coupling (i.e., AC vs ac). Parental coupling: ABC (A C), abc (a c). Recombinant classes: Abc (A c) -> recombinant; aBC (a C) -> recombinant; ABc (A c) -> recombinant; abC (a C) -> recombinant; AbC (A C) -> coupling; aBc (a c) -> coupling. So recombinant classes: Abc, aBC, ABc, abC = 80+80+70+70 = 300. RF_AC = 300/1000 = 0.30. Map distance ~30 cM.\n\nExplain why RF_AC \u2260 sum of adjacent distances (0.176+0.156=0.332). Because double crossovers restore parental configuration for outer markers, reducing observed recombination between them. Double crossovers (AbC and aBc) are counted as parental for A-C, thus subtract from total. Hence observed RF_AC is lower than sum of intervals.\n\nNow part 5: Expected number of double crossovers assuming independence of crossovers in two intervals. Probability of DCO = RF_AB * RF_BC (assuming small). More precisely, expected proportion = (RF_AB)*(RF_BC) ignoring multiple crossovers beyond one per interval. With total zygotes 1000, expected DCO = 1000 * 0.176 * 0.156 = 1000 * 0.027456 = 27.456 \u2248 27.5. Observed DCO count = sum of double crossover classes (AbC + aBc) = 8+8 = 16. Coefficient of coincidence = observed/expected = 16 / 27.456 = 0.583. Interference = 1 - 0.583 = 0.417 (~0.42).\n\nNow part 6: Ignore viability selection, use observed survivors directly (total 826). Compute apparent recombination fractions.\n\nTotal survivors N_obs = 826.\n\nCompute apparent RF_AB = (Abc + aBC + AbC + aBc)/826 = (80+80+8+4)/826 = 172/826 = 0.2083. Map distance ~20.8 cM.\n\nApparent RF_BC = (ABc + abC + AbC + aBc)/826 = (70+70+8+4)/826 = 152/826 = 0.1839 \u2192 18.4 cM.\n\nApparent RF_AC = (Abc + aBC + ABc + abC)/826 = (80+80+70+70)/826 = 300/826 = 0.3633 \u2192 36.3 cM.\n\nObserved DCO count (AbC + aBc) = 8+4 = 12. Expected DCO using apparent RFs: 0.2083*0.1839 = 0.0383. Expected number = 826*0.0383 = 31.66 \u2248 32. Coincidence = 12/31.66 = 0.379. Interference = 0.621.\n\nNow part 7: Compare corrected vs uncorrected results quantitatively.\n\nProvide table summarizing values.\n\nExplain distortion: Viability selection reduces survival of gametes that are a/a ; c/c (abc and aBc). This preferentially removes some recombinant classes more than others, altering observed frequencies. Specifically, the class abc (parental) loses half its individuals, decreasing apparent parental frequency and inflating recombinant proportion for intervals involving A-C? Actually abc is parental for all three loci, removal reduces parental count, making recombinant fractions larger when using raw survivors. Additionally, aBc is a recombinant class for A-C (since a and c separated) but also for B-C? It is recombinant for A-C (a B c) yes, and also for B-C? B and C are B c (recombinant). So removal of aBc reduces recombinant counts for both intervals, but effect differs. Overall, the bias leads to overestimation of recombination between A and B (apparent 0.208 vs corrected 0.176) and between B and C (0.184 vs 0.156) and especially between A and C (0.363 vs 0.30). The coefficient of coincidence appears lower (0.38 vs 0.58) indicating apparent increased interference due to selection mimicking fewer double crossovers.\n\nExplain that post-fertilization selection acts after meiosis, so it cannot be distinguished from meiotic recombination patterns; the reduced survival of certain genotypes mimics a reduction in production of those gametes, leading to misinterpretation as lower recombination.\n\nNow craft final answer with calculations shown step-by-step."
